WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.660
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Welcome
to "Deconstructing Chatbots."

00:00:02.660 --> 00:00:05.220
I'm Priyanka Vergadia,
and in this episode,

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:07.760
we will look at how to
help our ML algorithms

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:09.620
have better
conversations with users.

00:00:09.620 --> 00:00:12.375
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:17.290 --> 00:00:20.750
For us humans,
conversation is natural.

00:00:20.750 --> 00:00:22.690
It's a part of
our everyday life.

00:00:22.690 --> 00:00:26.440
We fundamentally understand it
and all the nuances around it

00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:29.390
because honestly, it's
part of who we are.

00:00:29.390 --> 00:00:32.500
This is why trying to teach a
machine to have a conversation

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:37.350
is so difficult. It's all
about conversation experience.

00:00:37.350 --> 00:00:39.715
OK, Google, what's the
weather like tomorrow?

00:00:39.715 --> 00:00:41.380
GOOGLE: In Mountain
View tomorrow,

00:00:41.380 --> 00:00:43.780
there will be scattered
showers with a high of 59

00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:44.799
and low of 46.

00:00:44.799 --> 00:00:46.840
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: How
you interact with machines

00:00:46.840 --> 00:00:48.650
right now seems pretty simple.

00:00:48.650 --> 00:00:51.010
You just ask something,
and the machine responds.

00:00:51.010 --> 00:00:53.440
But it turns out that this
is a really hard thing

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:56.530
to get right for a machine
since people ask for information

00:00:56.530 --> 00:00:58.190
in various different ways.

00:00:58.190 --> 00:01:00.850
For example, to do
something as simple

00:01:00.850 --> 00:01:03.020
as get the weather
you could say,

00:01:03.020 --> 00:01:05.830
what's the forecast
like today, or what's

00:01:05.830 --> 00:01:09.190
the weather right now,
or what's the temperature

00:01:09.190 --> 00:01:11.960
like in San Francisco tomorrow?

00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:14.980
Now, if you were to
code this traditionally,

00:01:14.980 --> 00:01:18.100
you'll most likely need a
whole matrix of conditionals

00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:22.420
to figure out all the edge cases
for all the ways your users can

00:01:22.420 --> 00:01:25.160
ask for this single information.

00:01:25.160 --> 00:01:28.750
But as you can imagine, that's
not really maintainable.

00:01:28.750 --> 00:01:32.770
This is where NLU or Natural
Language Understanding,

00:01:32.770 --> 00:01:34.480
comes in.

00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:38.460
NLU is a technology that
helps translate human language

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:41.490
into computer language
and vice versa.

00:01:41.490 --> 00:01:44.400
It's very similar to
Natural Language Processing,

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:48.090
but it goes a step further
to understand conversations

00:01:48.090 --> 00:01:50.430
that it hasn't been
trained to understand,

00:01:50.430 --> 00:01:55.050
like errors, spelling
mistakes, accents, sentiments,

00:01:55.050 --> 00:01:59.090
which makes NLU a
great fit for chatbots.

00:01:59.090 --> 00:02:01.170
And what's really
nice is that NLU

00:02:01.170 --> 00:02:04.150
works with both voice and text.

00:02:04.150 --> 00:02:07.480
Now, NLU is very similar
to Machine Learning,

00:02:07.480 --> 00:02:08.880
but it's not the same thing.

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:13.770
Rather, ML helps drive NLU by--

00:02:13.770 --> 00:02:16.080
actually, we'll talk about
this in a later episode.

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:18.430
Stay tuned for that.

00:02:18.430 --> 00:02:22.660
Now, a conversational experience
may not be on top of your list

00:02:22.660 --> 00:02:24.920
when you're thinking
about customer support,

00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:26.770
but it's actually
a critical element

00:02:26.770 --> 00:02:31.030
to meet customer expectations
in a personalized manner.

00:02:31.030 --> 00:02:35.260
And Google's Dialogflow
makes this possible.

00:02:35.260 --> 00:02:38.770
Dialogflow lets you build
conversational interfaces

00:02:38.770 --> 00:02:41.410
on top of your
products and services

00:02:41.410 --> 00:02:44.710
providing a powerful Natural
Language Understanding

00:02:44.710 --> 00:02:47.710
engine to process
and understand what

00:02:47.710 --> 00:02:49.690
your users are looking for.

00:02:49.690 --> 00:02:52.030
Which means when
you use Dialogflow,

00:02:52.030 --> 00:02:55.870
you get all the value of
NLU engine for your chatbots

00:02:55.870 --> 00:02:58.450
without having to lift a finger.

00:02:58.450 --> 00:03:01.580
Sadly, we are out of time for
this episode, but that's OK.

00:03:01.580 --> 00:03:03.340
We covered a lot of topics--

00:03:03.340 --> 00:03:06.550
conversational experiences,
why they are important,

00:03:06.550 --> 00:03:10.570
Natural Language Understanding,
and how Dialogflow lets you get

00:03:10.570 --> 00:03:12.700
up and running really quickly.

00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:14.980
Next, we are going to
learn some critical terms

00:03:14.980 --> 00:03:16.930
in the chatbot world
which will prepare

00:03:16.930 --> 00:03:20.050
us to go hands-on and
build an actual chatbot.

00:03:20.050 --> 00:03:22.300
If that sounds exciting
to you, join me

00:03:22.300 --> 00:03:24.520
in the next episode of
"Deconstructing Chatbots."

00:03:24.520 --> 00:03:27.570
[MUSIC PLAYING]

